-- Q1: medium / L2 — Lien arising automatically upon assessment and demand (timing focus)
(3, 'Tax Procedures', $EXPL$Redwood LLC has a $67,000 assessed tax deficiency. The IRS mailed a demand letter on March 1 but has not yet filed anything with the county recorder. As of March 15, does a federal tax lien exist on Redwood's assets?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Yes, the lien arose when the IRS assessed the tax and made the demand", "No, a lien requires the IRS to file a Notice of Federal Tax Lien first", "No, a lien requires a court order before attaching to property", "Yes, but only on real property until a public filing occurs"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Section 6321, a federal tax lien arises automatically upon assessment and demand for payment. No filing or court order is needed for the lien to exist — it immediately encumbers all of the taxpayer's property and rights to property. Wrong (B): Filing a Notice of Federal Tax Lien provides public notice and creditor priority but does not create the lien itself. Wrong (C): No court order is needed for a lien; court involvement relates to certain levy actions, not lien creation. Wrong (D): The statutory lien attaches to all property, both real and personal, upon assessment and demand.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q2: medium / L3 — Adequate disclosure negating extended SOL
(3, 'Tax Procedures', $EXPL$Bloom Architecture reported $600,000 gross income on its timely filed 2024 return. The firm omitted $180,000 from a design contract but included a detailed footnote explaining why it believed the income was excludable. Analyze whether the 6-year assessment period under Section 6501(e) applies.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The 6-year period applies because $180,000 exceeds 25% of $600,000", "The standard 3-year period applies because adequate disclosure negates the extension even though the omission exceeds 25%", "The IRS has an unlimited assessment period because the omission constitutes constructive fraud", "The 6-year period applies only if the omission exceeds 50% of gross income"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 6501(e), the 6-year statute applies when gross income omissions exceed 25%. Here, $180,000 exceeds 25% of $600,000 ($150,000). However, adequate disclosure of the omitted item on the return prevents the extended period from applying, limiting the IRS to the standard 3-year assessment window. Wrong (A): Although the 25% threshold is exceeded mathematically, adequate disclosure overrides the extension. Wrong (C): Disclosing an item and explaining the exclusion rationale does not constitute fraud, which requires intent to evade tax. Wrong (D): The statutory threshold is 25% of gross income, not 50%.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q3: hard / L4 — Court choice evaluation (all three factors align at District Court)
(3, 'Tax Procedures', $EXPL$Kaplan receives a $110,000 statutory notice of deficiency. She can prepay the full amount, wants a jury trial, and her attorney identifies favorable precedent only in her geographic circuit Court of Appeals. Evaluate which judicial forum best aligns with all of Kaplan's objectives.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Tax Court, because it follows geographic circuit precedent and has specialized tax judges", "Court of Federal Claims, because it handles government claims and Kaplan can prepay", "U.S. District Court, because it offers jury trials, accepts prepayment refund suits, and appeals to the geographic circuit with favorable precedent", "Tax Court Small Case Division, because simplified procedures benefit the taxpayer"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): U.S. District Court is the only court offering jury trials. Since Kaplan can prepay (required for District Court), and her geographic circuit has favorable precedent (where District Court appeals go), all three factors align. Wrong (A): Tax Court does not offer jury trials, which is one of Kaplan's stated objectives. Wrong (B): Court of Federal Claims does not offer jury trials and appeals to the Federal Circuit, not the geographic circuit with favorable precedent. Wrong (D): The Small Case Division is limited to disputes of $50,000 or less, does not offer jury trials, and the $110,000 deficiency exceeds the threshold.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q4: hard / L4 — Combined FTF/FTP penalty calculation (7 months late, non-fraudulent)
(3, 'Tax Procedures', $EXPL$Valdez files her return 7 months late with $20,000 of unpaid tax. The failure was not fraudulent. Evaluate the total combined penalty under Section 6651 for failure to file and failure to pay, given that the FTF penalty is reduced by the FTP amount during months both apply.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$5,000 — the FTF cap of 25% with no additional FTP penalty", "$7,000 — calculated as 25% FTF plus 10% FTP for the full 7 months", "$9,500 — the combined maximum of 47.5% of unpaid tax", "$5,200 — the FTF of 25% reduced by the 5-month FTP overlap, plus FTP of 0.5% for all 7 months"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Under Section 6651, the FTF penalty is 5% per month capped at 25% (reached at month 5). The FTP penalty is 0.5% per month. During the 5 months both apply, FTF is reduced by the FTP amount: net FTF = 25% minus 2.5% overlap = 22.5%. FTP runs for all 7 months: 0.5% x 7 = 3.5%. Total = 26% x $20,000 = $5,200. Wrong (A): This ignores the FTP penalty, which continues accruing for all 7 months. Wrong (B): The FTP rate is 0.5% per month, not 10% total, and the FTF cap is 25%, not additive with FTP at full rates. Wrong (C): The 47.5% combined maximum applies only when both penalties run to their full caps over many months, not after just 7 months.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q5: medium / L2 — Consent to extend statute of limitations (Form 872)
(3, 'Tax Procedures', $EXPL$Winslow Corp is under IRS examination with 4 months remaining on the 3-year statute of limitations. The revenue agent requests that Winslow sign Form 872 to extend the assessment period. What is the legal effect if Winslow agrees?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The statute of limitations is extended by the period specified in the consent agreement, giving the IRS additional time to complete the examination", "The statute of limitations is permanently waived and the IRS can assess at any time", "The extension applies only to penalties, not to the underlying tax assessment", "Signing Form 872 converts the examination into a criminal investigation"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Section 6501(c)(4), the taxpayer and IRS may agree in writing (Form 872) to extend the statute of limitations for a specified period. This gives the IRS additional time to complete the examination without losing assessment authority. The taxpayer benefits by preserving the right to an administrative resolution rather than receiving an immediate statutory notice of deficiency. Wrong (B): Form 872 extends the statute for a defined period — it does not permanently waive the limitations period. Wrong (C): The consent extends the assessment period for all taxes under examination, not just penalties. Wrong (D): A consent to extend has no connection to criminal proceedings; it is a routine administrative tool.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q6: easy / L1 — Correspondence audit characteristics
(3, 'Tax Procedures', $EXPL$Which IRS examination type involves sending a letter requesting documentation for a specific return item, with the taxpayer responding by mail?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Office audit", "Correspondence audit", "Field audit", "Compliance check"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): A correspondence audit is conducted entirely through the mail. The IRS sends a letter requesting documentation to support a specific line item, and the taxpayer responds by mailing the requested records. No in-person meeting occurs. Wrong (A): An office audit requires the taxpayer to visit an IRS office for an in-person meeting with an examiner. Wrong (C): A field audit is conducted at the taxpayer's home, business, or accountant's office by a revenue agent reviewing books and records. Wrong (D): A compliance check is not one of the three standard IRS audit types.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q7: medium / L2 — Math error authority (summary assessment without deficiency notice)
(3, 'Tax Procedures', $EXPL$The IRS identifies an arithmetic error on Kapoor's filed return that results in a $2,300 additional tax liability. Under Section 6213(b), what assessment procedure may the IRS use for this type of error?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The IRS must issue a statutory notice of deficiency and wait 90 days before assessing", "The IRS must first refer the case to the Appeals Office for review", "The IRS may summarily assess the additional tax without issuing a statutory notice of deficiency", "The IRS must obtain a court order from the Tax Court before adjusting the return"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Section 6213(b), the IRS has math error authority to summarily assess additional tax resulting from mathematical or clerical errors on a return without issuing a statutory notice of deficiency. The taxpayer receives a notice of the adjustment and may request abatement within 60 days. Wrong (A): The statutory notice of deficiency (90-day letter) is required for substantive disagreements, not for math errors, which can be corrected through summary assessment. Wrong (B): Math error assessments are administrative corrections that do not require Appeals Office involvement. Wrong (D): No court order is needed for math error assessments — they are routine IRS administrative corrections.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q8: easy / L1 — Burden of proof standard for civil fraud
(3, 'Tax Procedures', $EXPL$Under IRC Section 6663, what evidentiary standard must the IRS satisfy to impose the civil fraud penalty?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Probable cause", "Preponderance of the evidence", "Beyond a reasonable doubt", "Clear and convincing evidence"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Under IRC Section 6663, the IRS must prove fraud by clear and convincing evidence. This standard is higher than the general civil standard of preponderance but lower than the criminal standard of beyond a reasonable doubt. Wrong (A): Probable cause is a standard for search warrants and arrests, not for civil tax penalties. Wrong (B): Preponderance of the evidence is the standard for ordinary civil claims but is insufficient for civil fraud. Wrong (C): Beyond a reasonable doubt is the criminal standard that applies to criminal tax evasion under Section 7201, not civil fraud penalties.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q9: medium / L3 — Fraud burden shifting (entire underpayment treated as fraud)
(3, 'Tax Procedures', $EXPL$The IRS examines Thornton's return and proves by clear and convincing evidence that $30,000 of a total $80,000 underpayment is due to fraud. Thornton cannot demonstrate that the remaining $50,000 was not fraudulent. Analyze how the civil fraud penalty under Section 6663 applies to the full underpayment.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The 75% fraud penalty applies to the entire $80,000 because once the IRS proves any portion is fraudulent, the full underpayment is treated as fraud unless the taxpayer proves otherwise", "The 75% fraud penalty applies only to the $30,000 proven fraudulent, and the 20% accuracy penalty applies to the remaining $50,000", "The 75% fraud penalty applies only to the $30,000 and no penalty applies to the $50,000 because the IRS has the burden for each portion", "The 20% accuracy penalty applies to the entire $80,000 because the IRS proved fraud on less than half the underpayment"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Section 6663(b), if the IRS proves that any portion of an underpayment is due to fraud, the entire underpayment is treated as fraudulent unless the taxpayer establishes by a preponderance of evidence that specific portions are not due to fraud. Since Thornton cannot make this showing, the 75% penalty applies to the full $80,000. Wrong (B): The burden shifts to the taxpayer once the IRS establishes fraud on any portion — the IRS does not need to prove fraud dollar by dollar. Wrong (C): The IRS bears the initial burden, but once fraud is established on any portion, the taxpayer must prove the remainder was not fraudulent. Wrong (D): The accuracy-related penalty under Section 6662 cannot apply to any portion of an underpayment to which the fraud penalty applies.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q10: hard / L3 — Gross valuation misstatement (40% vs 20% penalty)
(3, 'Tax Procedures', $EXPL$Hartford donates equipment to a nonprofit and claims a $420,000 charitable deduction. An IRS examination determines the equipment's fair market value was $180,000. Analyze which accuracy-related penalty rate applies under Section 6662.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["No penalty applies because charitable donations are exempt from valuation penalties", "The 40% penalty for a gross valuation misstatement, because the claimed value exceeds 200% of the correct amount", "The standard 20% penalty for a substantial valuation misstatement at the 150% threshold", "The 75% civil fraud penalty because any overvaluation of more than 100% is presumed fraudulent"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 6662(h), a gross valuation misstatement exists when the claimed value is 200% or more of the correct amount. Hartford claimed $420,000 on equipment worth $180,000 — a ratio of 233%, exceeding the 200% gross misstatement threshold. The penalty rate increases from 20% to 40%. Wrong (A): Charitable contribution deductions are subject to the same valuation penalty rules as other deductions. Wrong (C): A substantial valuation misstatement (150%+) triggers a 20% penalty, but the 233% ratio here exceeds the 200% gross threshold, requiring the higher 40% rate. Wrong (D): An overvaluation alone does not establish fraud; the civil fraud penalty requires proof of intentional tax evasion by clear and convincing evidence.$EXPL$, 'hard', 3),

-- Q11: easy / L1 — Tax Court Small Case Division dollar threshold
(3, 'Tax Procedures', $EXPL$What is the dollar limit for electing the Tax Court Small Case Division under Section 7463?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$10,000 or less per tax year", "$25,000 or less per tax year", "$50,000 or less per tax year", "$100,000 or less per tax year"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Section 7463, the Tax Court Small Case Division handles disputes of $50,000 or less per tax year. These cases use simplified procedures but the decision is final and cannot be appealed by either party. Wrong (A): $10,000 is not a recognized threshold for Tax Court proceedings. Wrong (B): $25,000 is the threshold for requiring a formal written protest for IRS Appeals, not the Small Case Division limit. Wrong (D): $100,000 exceeds the Small Case Division threshold and would require the regular Tax Court division.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q12: medium / L2 — NFTL purpose (public notice and creditor priority)
(3, 'Tax Procedures', $EXPL$Stanton has $82,000 in assessed back taxes. After assessment and demand, the IRS files a Notice of Federal Tax Lien in the public records. What legal effect does this filing achieve?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["It creates the tax lien for the first time on Stanton's property", "It authorizes the IRS to garnish Stanton's wages immediately", "It converts the lien from a general encumbrance to a specific claim on identified assets", "It gives public notice of the existing lien and establishes the government's priority over most later creditors"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Filing a Notice of Federal Tax Lien (NFTL) provides public notice of the government's existing lien and establishes its priority position against subsequent purchasers, judgment lien creditors, and secured creditors. Without the NFTL, certain third parties may take priority over the IRS. Wrong (A): The lien already arose automatically upon assessment and demand under IRC Section 6321; the NFTL does not create it. Wrong (B): Wage garnishment is a type of levy action under Section 6331, requiring a separate Notice of Intent to Levy, not a lien filing. Wrong (C): The federal tax lien remains general — it attaches to all property and is not converted to a specific lien by the NFTL.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q13: easy / L2 — Field audit location (sole proprietor scenario)
(3, 'Tax Procedures', $EXPL$Nguyen operates a catering business and the IRS initiates a field audit of his Schedule C. At which location will the revenue agent most likely conduct the examination?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["At Nguyen's business location or his accountant's office", "At the nearest IRS district office", "Through a series of mailed correspondence", "At the regional Tax Court courthouse"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): A field audit is the most comprehensive IRS examination and is conducted at the taxpayer's home, place of business, or accountant's office. The revenue agent reviews books, records, and supporting documentation on-site. Wrong (B): Examinations conducted at an IRS office are called office audits, not field audits. Wrong (C): Examinations conducted entirely through the mail are correspondence audits, the simplest type. Wrong (D): The Tax Court is a judicial forum for litigating disputes, not a location where audits are conducted.$EXPL$, 'easy', 2),

-- Q14: hard / L4 — Penalty mutual exclusivity (6662 and 6663 cannot stack on same portion)
(3, 'Tax Procedures', $EXPL$The IRS determines that Boyle has a $120,000 underpayment. The IRS proves $70,000 is due to fraud under Section 6663 and asserts negligence under Section 6662 for the remaining $50,000. Boyle's attorney argues the 20% accuracy penalty should also apply to the $70,000 fraudulent portion. Evaluate whether both penalties can apply to the same dollars.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Both penalties apply to the $70,000 portion, resulting in a combined 95% penalty rate on that amount", "The accuracy-related penalty and fraud penalty cannot apply to the same portion of an underpayment — the 75% fraud penalty applies to the $70,000 and the 20% accuracy penalty applies only to the remaining $50,000", "The taxpayer can elect which penalty applies to each portion of the underpayment", "The fraud penalty replaces all other penalties on the entire $120,000 underpayment"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 6662(b), the accuracy-related penalty and the civil fraud penalty under Section 6663 cannot both apply to the same portion of an underpayment. The 75% fraud penalty applies to the $70,000 fraudulent portion, and the 20% accuracy penalty may apply to the separate $50,000 attributable to negligence. Wrong (A): Stacking both penalties on the same dollars is explicitly prohibited by statute — they are mutually exclusive for any given portion. Wrong (C): The taxpayer has no election right; the statute determines which penalty applies based on the nature of each portion of the underpayment. Wrong (D): The fraud penalty applies only to the portion proven fraudulent, not automatically to the entire underpayment when a separate non-fraud basis exists for the remainder.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q15: easy / L1 — Definition of a tax levy (seizure of property)
(3, 'Tax Procedures', $EXPL$What enforcement action allows the IRS to seize a taxpayer's property to satisfy an unpaid tax debt?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["A formal tax assessment", "A statutory notice of deficiency", "A tax levy on the taxpayer's assets", "A tax lien on the taxpayer's property"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Section 6331, a tax levy is the actual seizure of a taxpayer's property — including wages, bank accounts, and other assets — to satisfy a tax debt. The IRS must provide a Notice of Intent to Levy at least 30 days before seizing property. Wrong (A): A tax assessment is the formal recording of the tax liability on the IRS books, not the seizure of property. Wrong (B): A statutory notice of deficiency is a formal notice giving the taxpayer 90 days to petition the Tax Court, not a seizure action. Wrong (D): A tax lien is a legal claim or encumbrance on property, not the actual seizure — it secures the government's interest but does not take possession.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q16: easy / L1 — IRS interest rate adjustment frequency (quarterly)
(3, 'Tax Procedures', $EXPL$How often does the IRS adjust the interest rate charged on tax underpayments under IRC Section 6621?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Annually, on January 1 of each year", "Semiannually, on January 1 and July 1", "Monthly, based on Treasury auction results", "Quarterly, based on the federal short-term rate"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Under IRC Section 6621, the IRS underpayment interest rate is adjusted quarterly. The rate equals the federal short-term rate for the first month of each calendar quarter, plus 3 percentage points (or plus 5 for large corporate underpayments). Wrong (A): The rate is not fixed annually — it is recalculated each quarter to reflect current market conditions. Wrong (B): Semiannual adjustments are less frequent than the statutory quarterly schedule. Wrong (C): Monthly adjustments based on Treasury auctions are not the mechanism used for tax underpayment interest rates.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q17: medium / L2 — Hazards of litigation standard at Appeals
(3, 'Tax Procedures', $EXPL$Fernandez requests an Appeals conference over a proposed $45,000 deficiency. After reviewing the case, the Appeals officer estimates the IRS has a 35% chance of winning in court. What standard guides the officer's settlement authority?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The hazards of litigation, allowing the officer to settle based on the probability of the IRS prevailing at trial", "The ability-to-pay standard, requiring the officer to consider the taxpayer's financial condition", "The substantial authority standard, requiring at least 40% support for the IRS position", "The preponderance of evidence standard used in civil proceedings"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): IRS Appeals officers settle cases based on the hazards of litigation — the likelihood that the IRS would prevail if the case were tried in court. With a 35% chance of winning, the officer may concede a significant portion of the deficiency to reach a settlement. Wrong (B): The ability-to-pay standard governs offers in compromise and installment agreements, not merit-based settlements at Appeals. Wrong (C): Substantial authority is a standard for tax return positions under the penalty provisions, not a threshold for Appeals settlements. Wrong (D): Preponderance of evidence is a trial standard, not the framework used by Appeals officers to evaluate settlement terms.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q18: medium / L3 — Prepayment requirement comparison across three courts
(3, 'Tax Procedures', $EXPL$Albright receives a $95,000 statutory notice of deficiency. She does not wish to pay the disputed amount before challenging it in court. Analyze which judicial forums are available to Albright without prepayment and which require her to pay first.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["All three courts — Tax Court, District Court, and Court of Federal Claims — allow litigation without prepayment", "Only Tax Court allows Albright to litigate without prepayment; both District Court and Court of Federal Claims require full payment before filing a refund suit", "Only District Court allows litigation without prepayment because it has general civil jurisdiction", "Both Tax Court and Court of Federal Claims allow litigation without prepayment"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): The U.S. Tax Court is the only judicial forum that allows a taxpayer to challenge a deficiency without first paying the disputed tax. Both U.S. District Court and the Court of Federal Claims are refund courts, requiring the taxpayer to pay the full assessed amount and then sue for a refund. Wrong (A): District Court and Court of Federal Claims both require prepayment — they do not allow deficiency litigation. Wrong (C): District Court requires full prepayment; it is a refund forum, not a deficiency court. Wrong (D): Court of Federal Claims requires prepayment and is a refund forum, just like District Court.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q19: medium / L3 — Refund amount limited to lookback period
(3, 'Tax Procedures', $EXPL$Yoshi filed her 2021 tax return on April 15, 2022, paying $18,000 in tax at that time. On July 10, 2024, she made an additional $5,000 payment toward the same tax year. On May 1, 2025, she files a refund claim. Analyze the maximum refund Yoshi can recover under the lookback limitation of Section 6511.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$23,000, because both payments fall within the statutory refund period", "$18,000, because only the original payment qualifies and additional payments are not refundable", "$5,000, because the refund is limited to tax paid within 3 years before the claim and only the July 2024 payment falls within that window", "No refund, because the statute of limitations expired before the claim was filed"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Section 6511(b), when a refund claim is filed within 3 years of the return date, the refund is limited to tax paid within the 3-year period preceding the claim. Yoshi filed May 1, 2025; the lookback reaches May 1, 2022. The $18,000 paid April 15, 2022, falls before this date and is outside the window. The $5,000 paid July 10, 2024, is within the window and recoverable. Wrong (A): The $18,000 payment predates the 3-year lookback period and cannot be recovered. Wrong (B): Both payments are eligible in principle, but timing determines recoverability — the issue is the lookback window, not the nature of the payment. Wrong (D): The claim is timely because it was filed within 3 years of the original return date (April 15, 2022 to May 1, 2025).$EXPL$, 'medium', 3)
;

COMMIT;
